  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThe key idea is to exploit the two-scale structure inherent in the optimal solution for the minimum overlap problem. Recent numerical experiments suggest that the optimal h function forms a repeating pattern of dense and sparse regions. Our approach formalizes this by:\n1. Starting with an ensemble of locally optimal solutions at different scales\n2. Using a cross-scale interpolation scheme to transfer patterns between scales\n3. Employing a mixed-integer convex relaxation to enforce structure preservation\n\nThe method addresses the non-convex nature of the problem by leveraging the observation that the record-holding solution exhibits periodic structure at two different scales. This is a significant departure from continuous optimization approaches and may yield better results by respecting the natural periodicity of the optimal solution.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n-Two-scale representation: h(x) = a * h_fine(x) + (1-a) * h_coarse(x)\n-Objective minimization with constraints enforced via projected gradient descent\n-Heuristic relaxation: replace h_coarse with periodic sparse patterns\n-Feasibility maintenance through constrained programming\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n1. Initialize multi-scale ensemble:\n   - Generate base solution samples wihch=700 points\n   - Create coarse-gradients at scale factors s \u2208 [0.8, 0.9, 0.95]\n\n2. Scale interpolation:\n   for each scale factor s:\n      transfer h from base to new scale using non-uniform interpolation\n      apply periodic edge-smoothing\n      enforce integral constraint by projecting to feasible set\n\n3. Optimization phases:\n   Phase 1: SLSQP with multiscaling\n   Phase 2: CVXPY convexification with dual variables\n   Phase 3: Exact CD with periodicity constraints\n\n4. Feasibility checks throughout optimization:\n   - Continuous projection after each update\n   - Stochastic feasibility maintenance using redistibution kernel\n\n5. Deadline handling:\n   - Allocate 300s first to Phase 1\n   - Then 400s to Phase 2\n   - Finally 200s to Phase 3\n\n6. Convergence criteria:\n   - Reliability-based exit if no improvement in 150 iterations\n   - Time-based exit if ordered deadline reached\n   - Robustness threshold for candidate acceptance\n\nSearch Choices:\n-n_candidates = [0.85, 0.87, 0.89, 0.91]\n-lr_sweep = [0.01, 0.02, 0.04, 0.08]\n-feasibility_tol = 1e-6\n-initialization_mode = 'PeriodicRandom'\n\nComputational Budget:\nPrimary allocation: 1000 seconds\n-Phase1: Vectorized gradient descent (50% of time)\n-Phase2: Mixed-integer projection (25%)\n-Phase3: Cross-scale refinement (25%)\n\nValidation Contract:\n-All solutions must satisfy:\n  0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  |sum(h) - n/2| < 1e-4\n-Objectives computed via unrolled correlation\n-Fallback reverts to initial_h_values if infeasible found",
